  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 259 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThe fall chill will continue into Saturday before temperatures begin \nto moderate Sunday into next week. A long stretch of dry weather is \nexpected through much of next week. \n\nThe amplified trough shifts east of the area Friday night into \nSaturday with a longwave upper ridge over the western 2/3rds of the \nCONUS. Northwesterly flow continues aloft as the surface ridge moves \nsoutheastward and its orientation varies between RAP/NAM and long \nrange suites. These vary from a slanted ridge axis to a more \nformidable, consolidated surface high. Though minor, range solutions \nmay be the difference between a decent frost and cold, morning dew \non the lawn. The orientation, placement of the ridge, and resulting \nsurface flow suggest that while temperatures will be the coolest of \nthe season, so far, NBM is likely too cool, leaning at or just below \nthe 25th percentile of the IQR. LREF guidance has not only tightened \nin its IQR range (3-4 degrees), it has consistently shown morning \nlows in the upper 30s to low-40s with locally cooler temperatures in \nthe well-known microclimates (e.g. Freedom, and Rose Bud, MO). The \nscenario is slightly different for Sunday as the high positions \noverhead and winds become light and variable. Sunday looks like the \ncooler of the two, barring how quickly the high moves east and \nreturn flow commences. General speaking, morning lows in the low in \nthe upper 30s to low-40s will represent a large majority of the \narea. Locally cooler temperatures will support better frost \npotential with mid-30s.\n\nUpper ridging moves eastward and envelops the region Sunday into \nnext week. The pattern look void of any formidable systems and \ndrives the northern stream track into the northern CONUS/southern \nCanada. Another surface ridges moves west to east near the Canadian \nborder with dry easterly flow through the midweek period. NBM has \nsuggested high temperatures in the upper 70s to near 80 degrees \nthrough the midweek stretch, but has since come down to more \nreasonable levels (low to mid-70s). Morning lows will follow suit \nwith temperatures doing a daily stair step from the upper 30s/40s \nearly in the week to 50s by Friday.",
